daharness (0.1.0+pkg7) kali; urgency=high

  * purge lane (deb defect): a live purge round-trip on the VM lane
    (2026-10-20) left residue behind with dpkg "directory not empty so not
    removed" warnings on every module dir of /usr/share/daharness plus
    /etc/daharness — the package shipped NO postrm at all, and dpkg removes
    exactly the files in its manifest, never runtime-written state. Census
    of what survived: 98 untracked __pycache__/*.pyc across all module dirs
    (runtime imports bytecoded the root-owned tree; only vendor/ was
    precompiled at build), a stray ids.db at the tree root, /etc/daharness/
    pg.env + the postgres 18/daharness cluster still RUNNING and autostarted,
    the daharness system user/group (kali's granted membership included),
    and per-user lanes (~/.local/share/jupyter/kernels/daharness — a now
    broken zombie kernelspec — and ~/.local/share/framework-stubs/).
    Repairs, in three lanes: rules now compileall's the ENTIRE staging tree
    so runtime bytecode is dpkg-tracked (purge-clean, and the ~3-6x repeat
    import speedup the vendor-only compile gave boots now covers the whole
    tree); constants.resolve_workspace_root()/brain_socket_path() gained the
    pitch-decided fallback branches — the deb package tree
    (/usr/share/daharness) is state-hostile by name (root-run bare
    invocations can still write it, so writability alone is not enough) and
    non-writable trees fall through to /var/lib/daharness (socket lane:
    /run/daharness) with a loud log, never crashing a container lane whose
    state root is also unusable; daharness/findings.py FindingStore's
    default dropped its tree-relative _DEFAULT_DB (the actual ids.db-writer:
    utils/findings.py instantiates it bare at every tool call, which put
    findings state on the tree instead of the workspace root) for a
    workspace-resolved default. New debian/daharness.postrm: on purge only,
    rm -rf the residual tree, deluser the daharness account (guarded: our
    uid/home/shell shape), delgroup only-if-empty with a pointer when sudo
    members remain, and printed pointers for the intentional keeps —
    /var/lib/daharness work product, pg.env + the postgres cluster (drop
    with pg_dropcluster), and the per-user $HOME lanes (maintainer scripts
    never scrub homes). Man page: daharness-safety(7) gained the purge
    section with the same ledger + drop commands.
