daharness (0.1.0+pkg4) kali; urgency=high

  * selftest on the deb lane (VM 2026-10-20): a flag-only passthrough arg
    ('selftest -q', 'selftest -k chroma') stripped the tests/ anchor out of
    the pytest argv, so a bare pytest collected the whole rootdir INCLUDING
    the vendored closure's own test files — 21 collection errors, run
    interrupted (the root-owned tree also tripped pytest-cache Permission
    warnings, which read as the whole thing being a perms bug). The anchor
    is unconditional now (a passthrough test-file arg covers it), the
    cache provider is disabled on non-writable install trees, and the
    deb ships pyproject.toml so the pytest rootdir config (testpaths
    = tests) applies to any manual pytest too.
  * Selftest suite lands in the tree and SHIPS: the `selftest` REPL macro
    (pytest tests/) finally has a suite to drive — brain socket lane
    shaping (unit + real bind/cleanup cycle), findings-db probe truth
    (exception text surfacing + lane-block reporting), and the tool-author
    contract (argparse LOCAL_FILE mint + real subprocess run,
    _NO_LOCAL_TOOL opt-out, decorated function/method BRAIN_DISPATCH mint
    + in-process exec). tests/ installs under the framework root so the
    macro works on-box; pytest==9.1.1 rides the vendor pip line.
  * findings_db NAMEERROR fix carried by pkg3: its detail branch read
    `exc` from a bare `except Exception:` clause, so in the exact lane
    the pkg3 pass fixed to describe, framework_health would have crashed
    with a NameError instead of reporting. Now bound and regression-
    tested (tests/test_framework_health.py).
  * REPL selftest macro: honest EX_NOINPUT when a tree has no tests/
    directory, instead of pytest exit-4 usage noise.
  * The daharness CLI grew an argparse front door: --help/--daemon, a
    `doctor` subcommand that prints the framework_doctor report compactly
    (up/down per provider + copy-verbatim fixes; non-zero exit for
    scripting) and a prelaunch guard — when the services stack (Brain /
    API gateway / ZAP / msfrpcd) or its systemd daharness.service is
    already live, the CLI refuses with a clean remediation message (rc 78
    wedged-unit / rc 1 foreign leftovers) instead of racing the exact
    double-bind traceback seen with systemd enabled. INVOCATION_ID
    lets the unit lane skip the guard (it owns launch); `--force` is the
    explicit bypass.
  * CHROMA IS A DAEMON SIDECAR NOW — daharness-chroma.service is retired
    and merge-approved by the operator: the daemon's launch_all starts the
    registry backend FIRST (before Brain/ZAP), readiness-waits its lane,
    and reaps it with the generic sidecar shutdown (SIGTERM→grace→SIGKILL).
    Reuse-first: a lane already ANSWERING on CHROMA_HOST:CHROMA_PORT
    (compose container, dev-tree manual run) is adopted, never
    double-spawned. Motivation is the old unit's silent death: `chroma
    run` on an occupied port printed "Address localhost:9000 is not
    available" and EXITED 0, which Restart=on-failure recorded as success
    — the unit went quietly dead and the whole semantic lane (secretary
    search_tools, gateway find_best_tool, indexing, vector recall)
    degraded silently. The wrapper (packaging/bin/daharness-chroma) is
    now guarded for its remaining manual/dev role: refused rc 78 with
    stop-the-service / CHROMA_PORT / --force remediations when a lane
    answers, --help, and no consumed-flag passthrough. Absent-binary and
    wait-timeout are loud (chroma_backend_absent 'permanent answer',
    'SEMANTIC SEARCH IS DOWN' error naming doctor chroma), and
    framework_doctor gained a `chroma` provider.
  * Upgrade path: postinst stops+disables any still-RUNNING legacy
    daharness-chroma.service before the sidecar can boot (a removed unit
    file does not stop a running unit — it would keep holding :9000 for
    the sidecar's reuse-probe to adopt while systemd no longer supervises
    it).
  * daharness-pg init could NOT create the lane it promises (observed
    live on the guinea pig): every libpq call — the pg_database probe,
    createdb, run_sql, and the 001_roles.sh bundle's own psql — used
    libpq DEFAULT connection targets (:5432 + .s.PGSQL.5432, msfdb's
    'main' cluster) instead of the dedicated :5433 lane. With main down
    init died mid-way ("psql: error: connection to server on socket
    /var/run/postgresql/.s.PGSQL.5432 failed"); with main UP it would
    have silently built the harness db + lane roles inside the WRONG
    cluster. cmd_init now exports PGPORT="$PORT" before any libpq step
    (socket-name pinning keeps peer auth for the postgres superuser —
    no TCP + password trap), direct psql/createdb carry explicit
    -p "$PORT", and a failed pg_database probe DIES with the cluster
    name in the message instead of masquerading as "database missing"
    and falling into createdb against the same broken target.
    tests/test_daharness_pg.py pins the lane-address contract statically
    (suite: 68 passed).
  * pg init bundle: 004_operator_registry_acl.sql opened with a '#'
    header — a shell comment, not SQL — so the bundle's last step died
    under psql ON_ERROR_STOP=1 ("syntax error at or near '#'", observed
    live during the guinea-pig reinstall; roles mintered, schemas up,
    pg.env never written). Header is '--' now, and
    tests/test_daharness_pg.py forbids shell-style '#' lines in every
    init-bundle *.sql so a future header cannot re-kill the bundle's
    tail (suite: 69 passed).
  * framework_doctor gained a `postgres` provider: answers "can I use the
    engagement-cobweb Postgres lane" with one fixable story per state —
    tooling absent (apt + daharness-pg init / dev-lane dockered), init
    never run (sudo daharness-pg init), provisioned but down (sudo
    daharness-pg start) — port taken from $HARNESS_DB_PORT (default
    5433).

